{"toolCallId":"call_d9f85632b672480db7c4cf5f85730e38","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | [e.g., TypeScript, Python 3.11] |\n| **Framework** | [e.g., React, FastAPI] |\n| **Storage** | [e.g., PostgreSQL, None] |\n| **Testing** | [e.g., Jest, pytest] |\n\n### Overview\n[2-3 sentences: what this implements and the technical approach]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n- [ ] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [ ] Success criteria measurable\n- [ ] Dependencies identified\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] Tests passing (if applicable)\n- [ ] Docs updated (spec/plan/tasks)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n[MVC | MVVM | Clean Architecture | Serverless | Monolith | Other]\n\n### Key Components\n- **[Component 1]**: [Purpose]\n- **[Component 2]**: [Purpose]\n\n### Data Flow\n[Brief description of how data moves through the system]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n## FIX ADDENDUM: AFFECTED SURFACES\n\nUse this section when `research_intent=fix_bug`, when planning from a deep-review FAIL/CONDITIONAL verdict, or when any finding touches security, path handling, env precedence, schema boundaries, persistence, public responses, or shared policy.\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| [producer/helper/policy] | [what owns the behavior] | [update/unchanged/not a consumer] | [grep/test/doc evidence] |\n| [consumer/status/docs/tests] | [how it observes the behavior] | [update/unchanged/not a consumer] | [grep/test/doc evidence] |\n\nRequired inventories:\n- Same-class producers: `rg -n '<field|string|helper|literal|error-pattern>' <module-or-files>`.\n- Consumers of changed symbols: `rg -n '<changedSymbol>|<changedConstant>|<changedPublicField>' . --glob '*.ts' --glob '*.js' --glob '*.md'`.\n- Matrix axes: list every independent input axis and the required rows before implementation.\n- Algorithm invariant: for path/redaction/parser/resolver/security fixes, state the invariant and adversarial cases.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Unit | [Components/functions] | [Jest/pytest/etc.] |\n| Integration | [API endpoints/flows] | [Tools] |\n| Manual | [User journeys] | Browser |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| [System/Library] | [Internal/External] | [Green/Yellow/Red] | [Impact] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: [Conditions requiring rollback]\n- **Procedure**: [How to revert changes]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup) ──────┐\n                      ├──► Phase 2 (Core) ──► Phase 3 (Verify)\nPhase 1.5 (Config) ───┘\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Core, Config |\n| Config | Setup | Core |\n| Core | Setup, Config | Verify |\n| Verify | Core | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | [Low/Med/High] | [e.g., 1-2 hours] |\n| Core Implementation | [Low/Med/High] | [e.g., 4-8 hours] |\n| Verification | [Low/Med/High] | [e.g., 1-2 hours] |\n| **Total** | | **[e.g., 6-12 hours]** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n- [ ] Backup created (if data changes)\n- [ ] Feature flag configured\n- [ ] Monitoring alerts set\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n1. [Immediate action - e.g., disable feature flag]\n2. [Revert code - e.g., git revert or redeploy previous version]\n3. [Verify rollback - e.g., smoke test critical paths]\n4. [Notify stakeholders - if user-facing]\n\n### Data Reversal\n- **Has data migrations?** [Yes/No]\n- **Reversal procedure**: [Steps or \"N/A\"]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐\n│   Phase 1   │────►│   Phase 2   │────►│   Phase 3   │\n│   Setup     │     │    Core     │     │   Verify    │\n└─────────────┘     └──────┬──────┘     └─────────────┘\n                          │\n                    ┌─────▼─────┐\n                    │  Phase 2b │\n                    │  Parallel │\n                    └───────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| [Component A] | None | [Output] | B, C |\n| [Component B] | A | [Output] | D |\n| [Component C] | A | [Output] | D |\n| [Component D] | B, C | [Final] | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **[Phase/Task]** - [Duration estimate] - CRITICAL\n2. **[Phase/Task]** - [Duration estimate] - CRITICAL\n3. **[Phase/Task]** - [Duration estimate] - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: [Sum of durations]\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- [Task A] and [Task B] can run simultaneously\n- [Task C] and [Task D] can run after Phase 1\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | [Setup Complete] | [All dependencies ready] | [Date/Phase] |\n| M2 | [Core Done] | [Main features working] | [Date/Phase] |\n| M3 | [Release Ready] | [All tests pass] | [Date/Phase] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: [Decision Title]\n\n**Status**: [Proposed/Accepted/Deprecated]\n\n**Context**: [What problem we're solving]\n\n**Decision**: [What we decided]\n\n**Consequences**:\n- [Positive outcome 1]\n- [Negative outcome + mitigation]\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- [Option B]: [Why rejected]\n\n---\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Add one boolean flag to recommend-level.sh so text output collapses to the recommended-level line, without touching scoring, JSON output or exit codes.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag plan\"\n  - \"recommend-level.sh quiet implementation\"\n  - \"quiet flag shell script plan\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"implementation\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: plan-core | v2.2 -->\n# Implementation Plan: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n## 1. SUMMARY\n\n### Technical Context\n\n| Aspect | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Language/Stack** | Bash, macOS 3.2 compatible and Linux bash 5 |\n| **Framework** | None. One standalone script under the system-spec-kit runtime |\n| **Storage** | None |\n| **Testing** | `test-phase-system.js` and `test-phase-validation.js`, both Node scripts that shell out to the target |\n\n### Overview\n\nAdd a `QUIET` boolean beside `JSON_OUTPUT`, set it in the existing option parser, and return early from `output_text()` after printing the level line. The level line keeps its current text and its current place in the function, so there is one formatter rather than two.\n\nThe dispatch at the end of the script is untouched. `output_json()` is untouched. The only other edits are the two OPTIONS lists that document the flag.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n## 2. QUALITY GATES\n\n### Definition of Ready\n\n- [x] Problem statement clear and scope documented\n- [x] Success criteria measurable\n- [x] Dependencies identified\n- [x] Consumer inventory recorded in the affected-surfaces table below\n\n### Definition of Done\n\n- [ ] All acceptance criteria met\n- [ ] `--quiet` prints one line and exits 0 for every fixture in the test matrix\n- [ ] Default text output and JSON output diff clean against the captured baseline\n- [ ] Both suites report their baseline counts with the new case added\n- [ ] `validate.sh --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED` for this packet\n<!-- /ANCHOR:quality-gates -->\n\n---\n\n## AI EXECUTION PROTOCOL\n\n### Pre-Task Checklist\n\nBefore each task, read the target file section you are about to change, confirm the task is in scope, and name the command that will prove the task done. Do not edit `recommend-level.sh` and its test in the same step. Capture the baseline output first, because the diff is the only proof that default behavior survived.\n\n### Execution Rules\n\n| Rule | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| `TASK-SEQ` | Capture baselines, then edit the script, then edit the test, then diff and run both suites. |\n| `TASK-SCOPE` | Touch `recommend-level.sh` and `test-phase-system.js` only. No other script in `runtime/cli/spec/` changes. |\n| `TASK-EVIDENCE` | Report command output, not a summary of it. A passing suite means the printed count and exit code were read. |\n| `TASK-COMPAT` | Keep bash 3.2 compatibility. No associative arrays, no `mapfile`, no `${var,,}`. |\n\n### Status Reporting Format\n\nReport each task as `Status: <done|blocked> — Evidence: <command and result> — Next: <one action>`. Quote the exact quiet line once, with its byte count, rather than describing it.\n\n### Blocked Task Protocol\n\nIf the default output diff is non-empty, if a suite drops below its baseline, or if the quiet line differs from `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` by even a space, mark the task `BLOCKED`, record the observed output, and fix the cause before touching the test.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:architecture -->\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n### Pattern\n\nSingle-file CLI with an output selector. The script already chooses between two output functions at the end of `main`. The flag adds a third behavior inside the text branch rather than a third output function.\n\n### Key Components\n\n- **Option parser**: the `while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]` loop with its `case \"$1\"` block. `--quiet` joins `--json` here.\n- **State**: `QUIET=false` next to `JSON_OUTPUT=false`, in the configuration block above the helper functions.\n- **Text formatter**: `output_text()` prints the level line at its top. The quiet branch returns after that line.\n- **JSON formatter**: `output_json()` is not modified. It stays the machine-readable path.\n- **Help surfaces**: the file-header OPTIONS block and the `show_help` heredoc, which must agree.\n\n### Data Flow\n\n```text\nargv\n  │\n  ▼\noption parser ──► QUIET / JSON_OUTPUT / scoring inputs\n  │\n  ▼\nscoring ──► RECOMMENDED_LEVEL, LEVEL_NAME, phase fields\n  │\n  ▼\ndispatch\n  ├─ JSON_OUTPUT=true  ──► output_json()   (unchanged)\n  └─ JSON_OUTPUT=false ──► output_text()\n                            ├─ QUIET=true  ──► level line, return\n                            └─ QUIET=false ──► full report (unchanged)\n```\n<!-- /ANCHOR:architecture -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n## FIX ADDENDUM: AFFECTED SURFACES\n\nThis is a small behavior addition, not a defect fix. The table records every surface that reads the script so the blast radius is explicit.\n\n| Surface | Current Role | Action | Verification |\n|---------|--------------|--------|--------------|\n| `runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Producer of the text and JSON reports | Update | Diff default output, assert quiet output |\n| `tests/test-phase-system.js` | Invokes the script with `--json` and asserts phase fields | Update | Suite runs, count rises by one case |\n| `tests/test-phase-validation.js` | Invokes the script with `--json` over five fixtures | Unchanged | Suite still reports 51 passed |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-plan.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json --recommend-phases` | Unchanged | Command string still matches the help text |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-complete.yaml` | Calls the script with `--json --recommend-phases` | Unchanged | Command string still matches the help text |\n| `.skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-implement.yaml` | Mentions the script in a note only | Unchanged | No invocation to update |\n| `runtime/cli/retrieval/fixtures/phrase-variants.json` | Holds the script name as a retrieval phrase | Unchanged | Not an invocation |\n\nRequired inventories:\n\n- Same-class producers: `grep -rn 'recommend-level' --include='*.sh' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec` returns `recommend-level.sh` only. No second script prints a level line.\n- Consumers of changed symbols: the four invocations above are all `--json`. No caller parses the text output today, so `--quiet` cannot break an existing parser.\n- Matrix axes: two independent axes, output mode (`default`, `--no-recommend-phases`, `--json`, `--quiet`, `--quiet --json`) and flag position (`--quiet` first, last, repeated). Every cell has an expected line count.\n- Algorithm invariant: the recommended level, level name and confidence are computed before any formatter runs, so output mode cannot change them. The quiet line is the first line `output_text()` prints, which keeps it identical in both modes.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:affected-surfaces -->\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phases -->\n## 4. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nFollow the ordered tasks in `tasks.md`. It owns the Setup, Implementation and Verification phase checkboxes and task state.\n\nThe sequence is fixed by evidence order. Baselines are captured before the first edit, the script changes before the test, and the diff and suite runs close the phase. A test written against an unedited script proves nothing.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phases -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n## 5. TESTING STRATEGY\n\n| Test Type | Scope | Tools |\n|-----------|-------|-------|\n| Unit | The quiet line shape, the JSON precedence and the error path | New case in `test-phase-system.js` |\n| Regression | Default text output and JSON output over a fixed fixture set | `diff` against captured baselines in `scratch/` |\n| Suite | Phase field and fixture contracts for the script | `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-validation.js` |\n| Suite | Phase recommendation and parent append behavior | `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js` |\n| Manual | `--help` shows the flag, `--quiet --help` exits 0 | One shell invocation each |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n## 6. DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Dependency | Type | Status | Impact if Blocked |\n|------------|------|--------|-------------------|\n| Bash 3.2 or newer | External | Green | The flag syntax works on every bash the repo supports |\n| Node, for the two test suites | External | Green | Without it, only manual shell checks are possible |\n| `test-phase-system.js` baseline of 27 passed | Internal | Green | The count is the regression floor |\n| `test-phase-validation.js` baseline of 51 passed | Internal | Green | The count is the regression floor |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependencies -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:rollback -->\n## 7. ROLLBACK PLAN\n\n- **Trigger**: The default output diff is non-empty, or a suite drops below its baseline and the cause is the flag.\n- **Procedure**: Revert the two files to the previous revision. The change adds lines only, so reverting is a plain checkout of the two paths.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n## L2: PHASE DEPENDENCIES\n\n```\nPhase 1 (Setup: baselines) ──┐\n                             ├──► Phase 2 (Implementation) ──► Phase 3 (Verification)\nPhase 1.5 (Consumer scan) ───┘\n```\n\n| Phase | Depends On | Blocks |\n|-------|------------|--------|\n| Setup | None | Implementation |\n| Consumer scan | Setup | Implementation |\n| Implementation | Setup, Consumer scan | Verification |\n| Verification | Implementation | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:phase-deps -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:effort -->\n## L2: EFFORT ESTIMATION\n\n| Phase | Complexity | Estimated Effort |\n|-------|------------|------------------|\n| Setup | Low | 15 minutes |\n| Implementation | Low | 30 minutes |\n| Verification | Low | 30 minutes |\n| **Total** | | **About 1.5 hours** |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:effort -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n## L2: ENHANCED ROLLBACK\n\n### Pre-deployment Checklist\n\n- [ ] Baseline text and JSON output captured in `scratch/`\n- [ ] Both suite baselines recorded, 27 and 51 passed\n- [ ] No feature flag needed. The new behavior is opt-in by flag.\n\n### Rollback Procedure\n\n1. Revert `recommend-level.sh` to the previous revision.\n2. Revert `test-phase-system.js` to the previous revision.\n3. Run both suites and confirm 27 and 51 passed.\n4. Confirm `--quiet` is rejected again as an unknown option, which proves the revert landed.\n\n### Data Reversal\n\n- **Has data migrations?** No\n- **Reversal procedure**: N/A\n<!-- /ANCHOR:enhanced-rollback -->\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n## L3: DEPENDENCY GRAPH\n\n```\n┌──────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐\n│  Baseline capture│────►│  Script edit     │────►│  Diff and suites │\n│  text and JSON   │     │  flag and branch │     │  evidence        │\n└──────────────────┘     └────────┬─────────┘     └──────────────────┘\n                                  │\n                         ┌────────▼─────────┐\n                         │  Test case edit  │\n                         │  quiet assertion │\n                         └──────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Dependency Matrix\n\n| Component | Depends On | Produces | Blocks |\n|-----------|------------|----------|--------|\n| Baseline capture | None | `scratch/baseline-*.txt` | Script edit, diff |\n| Script edit | Baseline capture | Flag, help text, quiet branch | Test case edit, diff |\n| Test case edit | Script edit | Quiet assertion | Suite run |\n| Diff and suite run | Script edit, Test case edit | Closure evidence | None |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:dependency-graph -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n## L3: CRITICAL PATH\n\n1. **Capture baseline output** - 10 minutes - CRITICAL\n2. **Edit `recommend-level.sh`** - 25 minutes - CRITICAL\n3. **Edit `test-phase-system.js`** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n4. **Diff and run both suites** - 15 minutes - CRITICAL\n\n**Total Critical Path**: About 65 minutes.\n\n**Parallel Opportunities**:\n- The consumer scan can run while baselines are captured.\n- The help-text edit can run after the parser edit, since it touches no executable line.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:critical-path -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:milestones -->\n## L3: MILESTONES\n\n| Milestone | Description | Success Criteria | Target |\n|-----------|-------------|------------------|--------|\n| M1 | Baselines captured | Text and JSON output saved for the fixture set, both suite counts recorded | Phase 1 |\n| M2 | Flag works | `--quiet` prints one line and `--quiet --json` prints the full document | Phase 2 |\n| M3 | Proof recorded | Default output diff empty, both suites pass, packet validates | Phase 3 |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:milestones -->\n\n---\n\n## L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\n\n### ADR-001: `--quiet` governs text output only\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: The script has two output modes already. A caller can ask for human text or for a JSON document. A third flag has to say what it does when combined with `--json`, otherwise the two flags fight and the behavior depends on parse order.\n\n**Decision**: `--quiet` applies to text output. When `--json` is present, JSON wins and the flag has no effect on the document. The flag is still accepted, so a caller can pass both without an error.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: JSON keeps one stable shape for its four consumers.\n- Negative: `--quiet --json` does not shorten the output. Mitigated by documenting the precedence in the OPTIONS line rather than leaving it implicit.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- Minimal JSON under `--quiet`: it would add a second JSON shape, and every consumer would need to know which one it receives.\n- Rejecting the combination: it would turn a harmless pair of flags into a runtime error.\n\n### ADR-002: Reuse the existing level line\n\n**Status**: Accepted\n\n**Context**: The quiet output must be exactly the level line. Two ways exist to produce it. One adds an `output_quiet()` function with its own `echo`. The other returns early from `output_text()` after the existing `echo`.\n\n**Decision**: Return early from `output_text()`. The line text lives in one place.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Positive: A future change to the level line, such as adding a suffix, reaches both modes automatically.\n- Negative: `output_text()` carries one extra branch. It is a single comparison, and the function stays readable.\n\n**Alternatives Rejected**:\n- A separate `output_quiet()` function: two `echo` statements for one line invites drift, and the second one would not be covered by the existing text-mode reasoning.\n\n---\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":167,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":78}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/plan.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_da0b8be3072b47f69e2996dd812df788","title":"Ran ls, echo","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/spec-folder/generate-description.js 2>/dev/null && echo present; ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/graph/backfill-graph-metadata.js 2>/dev/null && echo present2","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/spec-folder/generate-description.js 2>/dev/null && echo present; ls .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/graph/backfill-graph-metadata.js 2>/dev/null && echo present2","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["ls","echo"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_dbfa99f06f18470489a08a9659c02f2f","title":"Read lines 80-220 in ./.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/graph/generated-metadata-drift.js","kind":"execute","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"resource","resource":{"mimeType":"text/x-shellscript","text":"sed -n '80,220p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/graph/generated-metadata-drift.js","uri":"tool://preview"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/preview_is_shell_command":true}}}],"rawInput":{"command":"sed -n '80,220p' .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/lib/graph/generated-metadata-drift.js","workdir":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/commandNames":["sed"],"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_dc2a3b6afc6747849dcf765d2b6c9cf7","title":"Edit file","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Give recommend-level.sh a --quiet flag so a caller can read the recommended level from one line instead of parsing the human-readable breakdown. Default output stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"recommend-level.sh quiet output\"\n  - \"recommend level single line\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"implementation\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a 12 to 16 line report built for a human reader. A script that only wants the level has to grep the second line or parse the JSON document. `tail -1` does not work, because the phase section and the score line follow the level line. `--quiet` adds an output mode that prints the level line and nothing else.\n\nThe change touches one script and one test file. It adds one state variable, one branch in the option parser, one early return in `output_text()`, and one entry in each of the two OPTIONS lists.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` changes text output only, so `--json` keeps its full document. The level line keeps coming from the existing `output_text()` code path instead of a second formatter that could drift.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: The two test suites that invoke this script, `test-phase-system.js` and `test-phase-validation.js`, plus the two speckit command assets that call it with `--json`.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n| **Affected file** | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` |\n| **Estimated change** | 10 to 14 added lines, no deleted lines |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nThe text output of `recommend-level.sh` opens with a blank line, then the level line, then the score line, then a breakdown and a phase section. Measured on 2026-10-26 with `--loc 200 --files 5 --api`, that is 16 lines. With `--no-recommend-phases` it is 12. Nothing in the output is designed for a caller that only needs the number, so every caller writes its own parser. The JSON mode exists for that need, but it returns a 32 to 36 line document, which is a heavy dependency for one integer and one label.\n\n### Purpose\n\nA caller that passes `--quiet` receives the recommended level on a single line and needs no parser.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Accept `--quiet` as a boolean flag in the option parser of `recommend-level.sh`.\n- Print only the recommended-level line in text mode when the flag is set.\n- Document the flag in both OPTIONS lists, the file header block and the `show_help` heredoc.\n- Add one regression case to `test-phase-system.js` that asserts the quiet output shape.\n- Prove the default text output and the JSON output are unchanged.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- A `-q` short alias. The task names `--quiet` only, so the short form stays free until a caller asks for it.\n- Suppressing fields inside the JSON document. JSON already has one consumer shape and truncating it would break machine readers.\n- Any change to scoring, thresholds, level mapping, confidence or phase recommendation.\n- Any change to `create.sh`, `validate.sh` or the other scripts in the same directory.\n- A new log level, a `--verbose` counterpart or a general output-format refactor.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, its help text and the quiet branch in `output_text()` |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js` | Modify | Add a quiet-output case beside the existing recommend-level fixtures |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The script accepts `--quiet` as a boolean flag that takes no argument, and accepts it anywhere in the argument list. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet` and no `--json`, stdout carries exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and nothing else. No leading blank line, no score line, no breakdown and no phase section. |\n| REQ-003 | Without `--quiet`, text output and JSON output are byte-identical to the current script for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet` changes what is printed, not what is computed. Score, recommended level, level name, confidence and phase fields keep their current values. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-005 | `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`. Passing both prints the full JSON document, so a machine reader never receives a truncated object. |\n| REQ-006 | Error paths keep their current stderr text and exit code 1 under `--quiet`. The flag never suppresses a diagnostic. |\n| REQ-007 | `--quiet` appears in the file-header OPTIONS block and in the `show_help` OPTIONS list, with matching wording. `--quiet --help` still prints the full help text and exits 0. |\n| REQ-008 | Both recommend-level suites keep their baseline counts, `test-phase-system.js` at 27 passed and `test-phase-validation.js` at 51 passed, and a new case asserts the quiet shape: one line, exact text, exit 0. |\n| REQ-009 | The change adds no new process, no new dependency and no file beyond the one test edit. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --loc 200 --files 5 --api --quiet` prints one line, exits 0 and leaves stderr empty.\n- **SC-002**: A captured before and after diff of the default text output and of the JSON output over a fixed fixture set is empty.\n- **SC-003**: An operator can find the flag in `--help` without opening the script.\n- **SC-004**: The phase-system suite reports the 27 baseline cases plus the new quiet case, with 0 failures.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | The phase-system suite is the only automated consumer, and it reads JSON only. | A text-mode regression has no existing coverage. | Add the quiet case in the same file and capture the default text output before the edit. |\n| Risk | A careless guard suppresses the wrong lines. | Existing human readers lose the breakdown. | Prove the default path with a byte-level diff, not by eye. |\n| Risk | The flag is read after the output dispatch instead of before it. | `--quiet` is accepted but has no effect. | Parse it in the existing `while` loop, next to `--json`, and assert the effect in a test. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: The flag adds no measurable runtime. It is one shell comparison on an already parsed argument list, in a script that finishes in milliseconds.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: No new input surface. `--quiet` takes no value, so it cannot introduce a parsing or injection path.\n- **NFR-S02**: Error diagnostics stay on stderr and keep their text, so a quiet run cannot hide a bad argument from a log.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: The same arguments produce the same output on every run. The flag reads no environment variable and no clock.\n- **NFR-R02**: The script keeps `set -euo pipefail` and its current exit codes, so a caller's error handling does not change.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data and Runtime Boundaries\n\n- **Given** the default phase section is on, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the phase section is not printed at all.\n- **Given** `--no-recommend-phases`, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the output is the same single line as the default case.\n- **Given** `--quiet` before the other flags, **When** the script runs, **Then** the result matches the case where it comes last.\n- **Given** `--quiet` appears twice, **When** the script runs, **Then** it is accepted and the output is the same single line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- **Given** no `--loc` and no `--files`, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the error text and exit code 1 are unchanged.\n- **Given** `--json` and `--quiet` together, **When** the script runs, **Then** the full JSON document is printed and no level line is added.\n- **Given** `--help` with `--quiet`, **When** the script runs, **Then** help prints and the exit code is 0.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 8/25 | Files: 2, LOC: about 14 added, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 5/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N |\n| Research | 5/20 | One script read, one test file read, no external source |\n| Multi-Agent | 2/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 4/15 | Dependencies: the test suite baseline |\n| **Total** | **24/100** | **Level 3 by operator instruction** |\n\nThe score describes a small change. The packet is Level 3 because the operator set that level when scaffolding it, not because the work scores high. The extra sections record the verification and rollback that a shipped CLI flag needs.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The default text output drifts while adding the quiet branch. | High | Low | Capture the current output first, diff it after, and keep the level line in one place. |\n| R-002 | `--quiet --json` is read as \"print one JSON line\". | Medium | Medium | Fix the precedence in REQ-005 and assert it in the new test case. |\n| R-003 | The quiet path ships without a regression test because the suite is JSON-only. | Medium | High | Add the case in `test-phase-system.js` in the same change. |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Read the Level From One Line (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** script author, **I want** `--quiet` to print only the recommended level, **so that** I can capture it with `$(...)` and no parsing.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows AC-001, AC-002, AC-004).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Keep the Existing Report (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** maintainer, **I want** the default output to stay unchanged, **so that** existing logs, docs and test fixtures remain valid.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-003).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Find the Flag in Help (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator, **I want** `--quiet` documented in `--help`, **so that** I can discover it without reading the script.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-007).\n\n---\n\n### US-004: Trust the Flag to Stay (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** test owner, **I want** a regression case for the quiet shape, **so that** a later refactor cannot drop the flag without a failure.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-008).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\nNone open at planning time. Three were settled while writing this specification:\n\n- Whether `--quiet` should also cover JSON. It does not, because JSON already has a stable consumer shape. Recorded in REQ-005 and in `plan.md` ADR-001.\n- Whether the quiet line should come from a new formatter. It should not, because a second formatter is a second place for the line text to drift. Recorded in `plan.md` ADR-002.\n- Whether `--quiet` should disable phase scoring. It should not. The flag controls printing, and computing the phase result costs nothing a caller can observe.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Closure Gate**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n- **Decisions**: See `plan.md` ADR-001 and ADR-002\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Give recommend-level.sh a --quiet flag so a caller can read the recommended level from one line instead of parsing the human-readable breakdown. Default output stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\n  - \"recommend-level.sh quiet output\"\n  - \"recommend level single line\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"implementation\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.2 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a 12 to 16 line report built for a human reader. A script that only wants the level has to grep the second line or parse the JSON document. `tail -1` does not work, because the phase section and the score line follow the level line. `--quiet` adds an output mode that prints the level line and nothing else.\n\nThe change touches one script and one test file. It adds one state variable, one branch in the option parser, one early return in `output_text()`, and one entry in each of the two OPTIONS lists.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` changes text output only, so `--json` keeps its full document. The level line keeps coming from the existing `output_text()` code path instead of a second formatter that could drift.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: The two test suites that invoke this script, `test-phase-system.js` and `test-phase-validation.js`, plus the two speckit command assets that call it with `--json`.\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-26 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n| **Affected file** | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` |\n| **Estimated change** | 10 to 14 added lines, no deleted lines |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\nThe text output of `recommend-level.sh` opens with a blank line, then the level line, then the score line, then a breakdown and a phase section. Measured on 2026-10-26 with `--loc 200 --files 5 --api`, that is 16 lines. With `--no-recommend-phases` it is 12. Nothing in the output is designed for a caller that only needs the number, so every caller writes its own parser. The JSON mode exists for that need, but it returns a 32 to 36 line document, which is a heavy dependency for one integer and one label.\n\n### Purpose\n\nA caller that passes `--quiet` receives the recommended level on a single line and needs no parser.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n\n- Accept `--quiet` as a boolean flag in the option parser of `recommend-level.sh`.\n- Print only the recommended-level line in text mode when the flag is set.\n- Document the flag in both OPTIONS lists, the file header block and the `show_help` heredoc.\n- Add one regression case to `test-phase-system.js` that asserts the quiet output shape.\n- Prove the default text output and the JSON output are unchanged.\n\n### Out of Scope\n\n- A `-q` short alias. The task names `--quiet` only, so the short form stays free until a caller asks for it.\n- Suppressing fields inside the JSON document. JSON already has one consumer shape and truncating it would break machine readers.\n- Any change to scoring, thresholds, level mapping, confidence or phase recommendation.\n- Any change to `create.sh`, `validate.sh` or the other scripts in the same directory.\n- A new log level, a `--verbose` counterpart or a general output-format refactor.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, its help text and the quiet branch in `output_text()` |\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js` | Modify | Add a quiet-output case beside the existing recommend-level fixtures |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | The script accepts `--quiet` as a boolean flag that takes no argument, and accepts it anywhere in the argument list. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet` and no `--json`, stdout carries exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and nothing else. No leading blank line, no score line, no breakdown and no phase section. |\n| REQ-003 | Without `--quiet`, text output and JSON output are byte-identical to the current script for the same arguments. |\n| REQ-004 | `--quiet` changes what is printed, not what is computed. Score, recommended level, level name, confidence and phase fields keep their current values. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-005 | `--json` takes precedence over `--quiet`. Passing both prints the full JSON document, so a machine reader never receives a truncated object. |\n| REQ-006 | Error paths keep their current stderr text and exit code 1 under `--quiet`. The flag never suppresses a diagnostic. |\n| REQ-007 | `--quiet` appears in the file-header OPTIONS block and in the `show_help` OPTIONS list, with matching wording. `--quiet --help` still prints the full help text and exits 0. |\n| REQ-008 | Both recommend-level suites keep their baseline counts, `test-phase-system.js` at 27 passed and `test-phase-validation.js` at 51 passed, and a new case asserts the quiet shape: one line, exact text, exit 0. |\n| REQ-009 | The change adds no new process, no new dependency and no file beyond the one test edit. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --loc 200 --files 5 --api --quiet` prints one line, exits 0 and leaves stderr empty.\n- **SC-002**: A captured before and after diff of the default text output and of the JSON output over a fixed fixture set is empty.\n- **SC-003**: An operator can find the flag in `--help` without opening the script.\n- **SC-004**: Both suites report their baseline plus the new quiet case, 27 and 51 passing before the change and 28 and 51 after, with 0 failures.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | The phase-system suite is the only automated consumer, and it reads JSON only. | A text-mode regression has no existing coverage. | Add the quiet case in the same file and capture the default text output before the edit. |\n| Risk | A careless guard suppresses the wrong lines. | Existing human readers lose the breakdown. | Prove the default path with a byte-level diff, not by eye. |\n| Risk | The flag is read after the output dispatch instead of before it. | `--quiet` is accepted but has no effect. | Parse it in the existing `while` loop, next to `--json`, and assert the effect in a test. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n\n- **NFR-P01**: The flag adds no measurable runtime. It is one shell comparison on an already parsed argument list, in a script that finishes in milliseconds.\n\n### Security\n\n- **NFR-S01**: No new input surface. `--quiet` takes no value, so it cannot introduce a parsing or injection path.\n- **NFR-S02**: Error diagnostics stay on stderr and keep their text, so a quiet run cannot hide a bad argument from a log.\n\n### Reliability\n\n- **NFR-R01**: The same arguments produce the same output on every run. The flag reads no environment variable and no clock.\n- **NFR-R02**: The script keeps `set -euo pipefail` and its current exit codes, so a caller's error handling does not change.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data and Runtime Boundaries\n\n- **Given** the default phase section is on, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the phase section is not printed at all.\n- **Given** `--no-recommend-phases`, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the output is the same single line as the default case.\n- **Given** `--quiet` before the other flags, **When** the script runs, **Then** the result matches the case where it comes last.\n- **Given** `--quiet` appears twice, **When** the script runs, **Then** it is accepted and the output is the same single line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n\n- **Given** no `--loc` and no `--files`, **When** `--quiet` is passed, **Then** the error text and exit code 1 are unchanged.\n- **Given** `--json` and `--quiet` together, **When** the script runs, **Then** the full JSON document is printed and no level line is added.\n- **Given** `--help` with `--quiet`, **When** the script runs, **Then** help prints and the exit code is 0.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 8/25 | Files: 2, LOC: about 14 added, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 5/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N |\n| Research | 5/20 | One script read, one test file read, no external source |\n| Multi-Agent | 2/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 4/15 | Dependencies: the test suite baseline |\n| **Total** | **24/100** | **Level 3 by operator instruction** |\n\nThe score describes a small change. The packet is Level 3 because the operator set that level when scaffolding it, not because the work scores high. The extra sections record the verification and rollback that a shipped CLI flag needs.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The default text output drifts while adding the quiet branch. | High | Low | Capture the current output first, diff it after, and keep the level line in one place. |\n| R-002 | `--quiet --json` is read as \"print one JSON line\". | Medium | Medium | Fix the precedence in REQ-005 and assert it in the new test case. |\n| R-003 | The quiet path ships without a regression test because the suite is JSON-only. | Medium | High | Add the case in `test-phase-system.js` in the same change. |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: Read the Level From One Line (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** script author, **I want** `--quiet` to print only the recommended level, **so that** I can capture it with `$(...)` and no parsing.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows AC-001, AC-002, AC-004).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Keep the Existing Report (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** maintainer, **I want** the default output to stay unchanged, **so that** existing logs, docs and test fixtures remain valid.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-003).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Find the Flag in Help (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator, **I want** `--quiet` documented in `--help`, **so that** I can discover it without reading the script.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-007).\n\n---\n\n### US-004: Trust the Flag to Stay (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** test owner, **I want** a regression case for the quiet shape, **so that** a later refactor cannot drop the flag without a failure.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (row AC-008).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\nNone open at planning time. Three were settled while writing this specification:\n\n- Whether `--quiet` should also cover JSON. It does not, because JSON already has a stable consumer shape. Recorded in REQ-005 and in `plan.md` ADR-001.\n- Whether the quiet line should come from a new formatter. It should not, because a second formatter is a second place for the line text to drift. Recorded in `plan.md` ADR-002.\n- Whether `--quiet` should disable phase scoring. It should not. The flag controls printing, and computing the phase result costs nothing a caller can observe.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Closure Gate**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n- **Decisions**: See `plan.md` ADR-001 and ADR-002\n\n---\n\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":1,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":1}}],"locations":[{"path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w4fbf614733/tree/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"edit"}}
